From f6d12b6362b26dd405c028fad8e60c48182f2e34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:11:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(api): sell metered access self-serve, and fix two live
 deployment bugs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0014. Patch 0013 built a machine interface nobody could buy: /developers
documented it and then routed the reader to a free qualifier. A product that
cannot be purchased at 2am by a stranger is not a product, it is a brochure.

SELF-SERVE METERED ACCESS

Three monthly plans on /developers#plans — Triage €900/600 units, Scale
€2,900/2,500, Platform €7,500/10,000. Checkout runs in subscription mode; the
webhook opens an account and emails a link where the key is minted and shown
once. No human in the path. A key that arrives tomorrow is a lost customer.

No overage billing. Over the allowance the engine returns 402 and they upgrade or
wait for the reset — an invoice a customer did not expect costs more than the
units would have earned.

KEYS THAT VERIFY OFFLINE

gfk1.<payload>.<signature>: account, key id, quota, scope, issue and expiry dates,
HMAC-SHA256 with GRIDFORGE_KEY_SECRET. The engine verifies arithmetic it can do
itself — no database, no network call, no latency floor, and a stateless container
stays stateless. The cost of statelessness is revocation, paid two ways: 35-day
expiry (not 30, so a renewal on the 1st never leaves a customer's agents dark
while a webhook lands) and GRIDFORGE_REVOKED_KEYS for what cannot wait, which is
why the payload carries a short id rather than needing the whole key pasted into a
deny list.

We never store a key. The database holds the id of the live one and the ids we
have revoked. A database that can hand somebody a working credential is a database
worth stealing, and there is no reason for this one to be.

The website mints (lib/api-access.ts) and the engine verifies
(gridforge/api/keys.py) with no shared runtime between them, so the test mints a
key in Node, verifies it in Python, and asserts both produce the identical string.
A divergence would not fail loudly — it would issue keys that quietly do not work,
to customers who have just paid.

Usage aggregates under the account, not the key: rotating mid-month must not hand
out a second free allowance.

Static GRIDFORGE_API_KEYS still authenticate and still win over signed keys. An
operator pasting a key into the environment at 2am to get a customer working again
must not be overridden by anything cleverer.

TWO BUGS THAT WERE LIVE

1. deploy-engine.sh rotated the production API key on EVERY run. Each deploy
   invalidated the key in Vercel, and paid deliverable generation returned 401
   until somebody noticed and re-pasted it. It also re-created the app each time,
   because it grepped `fly apps list` output whose format has changed — which is
   the error visible in the last deploy log. The script now asks the app about
   itself, adds only missing secrets, and prints <unchanged> rather than a new
   key. A deploy script that breaks production on success is worse than one that
   fails, because nothing tells you.

2. GRIDFORGE_USAGE_FILE=/data/usage.json with no volume mounted discarded every
   usage record — with min_machines_running = 0, every few minutes — and looked
   identical to a working configuration until the first invoice. fly.toml now
   declares the mount, the deploy script creates the volume, and the meter probes
   the path at startup: if it cannot write, /v1/usage says so instead of
   reporting durable:true on a path that silently throws everything away.

ALSO

  * tests/test_catalogue_parity.py — commercial.py has claimed since patch 0001
    that this test existed. It did not. It parses lib/products.ts and fails when
    the engine's prices and the checkout page's disagree.
  * /admin/pipeline shows API accounts, units sold, combined ARR, and prints the
    exact fly secrets command for the revocation list.
  * 401 now distinguishes expired, revoked and forged. "Invalid key" sends an
    integrator hunting a typo when their subscription lapsed three days ago.
  * auth_configured is true when GRIDFORGE_KEY_SECRET alone is set, so a
    self-serve deployment holding none of its customers' keys does not report
    itself broken.
  * robots.txt disallows every token-addressed path. A crawler indexing one
    publishes a customer's deliverable to anyone who searches for it.
  * The cross-language test was written against literal dates and would have
    started failing on expiry next month, looking exactly like the divergence it
    exists to catch. Fixed to relative dates before it shipped.

TESTS

27 new (249 total). A tampered payload, a wrong secret, an expired key, a revoked
key and six kinds of junk are all refused; pinning GRIDFORGE_REPORT_DATE cannot
extend a key's life; a key the engine has never seen works over HTTP; rotation
does not reset the allowance; and the price lists cannot drift.

tsc clean, next lint clean, next build clean with /developers, /api-access/[token]
and /admin/pipeline in the route table. Reference artefacts unchanged.

